# Assignment 2.4 — HTML Validation & Debugging

## Objective

To identify and fix errors in an HTML file, inspect the page using browser Developer Tools, validate the HTML markup, and organize the corrected code with proper indentation.

## Solution

First, I created a sample HTML file containing intentional errors such as missing closing tags, incorrect nesting of elements, missing attributes, and improper indentation.

Next, I opened the HTML file in a web browser and used Developer Tools by pressing F12. In the Elements panel, I inspected the HTML structure and identified problems with the markup. I also checked the browser console for any related warnings or errors.

Then, I used the W3C Markup Validation Service (https://validator.w3.org/) to validate the HTML code and identify markup errors.

After identifying the errors, I corrected the missing closing tags, fixed the incorrect nesting of elements, added the required attributes, and organized the code using proper indentation. Finally, I reopened the corrected HTML file in the browser and verified that the webpage displayed correctly.

## 1. Before: Buggy HTML Code

The following HTML code contains intentional errors for debugging practice.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>My Webpage
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to My Website
    <p>This is my first paragraph.
    <div>
        <p>This is some content.
    </p></div>
    <img src="photo.jpg">
    <a href="https://example.com">Visit Website
</body>
</html>
```

## 2. Errors Identified

The following errors were identified in the original HTML code:

1. **Missing closing title tag:** The `title` element was not properly closed before the `head` element ended.
2. **Missing closing heading tag:** The `h1` element did not have a closing `h1` tag.
3. **Missing closing paragraph tag:** The first `p` element was not explicitly closed.
4. **Incorrect nesting:** The paragraph and `div` elements were arranged in a confusing and improperly nested structure.
5. **Missing image alt attribute:** The `img` element did not include an `alt` attribute describing the image.
6. **Missing closing anchor tag:** The `a` element was not properly closed.
7. **Poor indentation:** The code was not consistently indented, making it harder to read and debug.
8. **Missing language and character encoding declarations:** The HTML document did not specify the page language or character encoding.

## 3. After: Corrected HTML Code

The following code fixes the identified problems and uses proper indentation.

Save this code as `index.html`.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>My Webpage</title>
</head>
<body>
    <!-- Main heading -->
    <h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>

    <!-- First paragraph -->
    <p>This is my first paragraph.</p>

    <!-- Content section -->
    <div>
        <p>This is some content.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Image with alternative text -->
    <img src="photo.jpg" alt="A sample photograph">

    <!-- External link -->
    <p>
        <a href="https://example.com">Visit Website</a>
    </p>
</body>
</html>
```

**Note:** If you want the image to appear in the browser, place an image named `photo.jpg` in the same folder as `index.html`. Otherwise, replace the image filename with the path to an existing image.

## 4. Inspecting HTML Using Browser DevTools

I followed these steps to inspect the webpage:

1. Opened `index.html` in Google Chrome.
2. Pressed F12 to open Developer Tools.
3. Selected the Elements panel.
4. Inspected the HTML elements and their nesting.
5. Compared the original code with the corrected structure.
6. Verified that the heading, paragraphs, image element, and link were present.

The Elements panel displays the browser's parsed DOM structure. Because browsers automatically repair some HTML errors, the Elements panel may not show the original source exactly as written.

